    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2024/12/saucony-endorphin-trainer-review-2025.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: Soft IncrediRun over firmer PWRRUN gives a balanced, non-mushy feel with strong impact protection from the high stack, though the rearfoot can feel slightly clunky while the forefoot is stiffer.",
        "Responsiveness: The 3/4 carbon plate plus IncrediRun produce a snappy, moderately bouncy ride with better energy return at uptempo/workout paces than easy runs.",
        "Stability: The shoe is stable-neutral with a stiff platform (limited forefoot flexibility), a moderately stiff/rigid heel counter, and lateral/medial sole flare that improves support but can feel laterally biased in the heel.",
        "Durability: Outsole coverage improves durability versus Kinvara Pro, but early posterior-lateral heel wear was noted and overall durability trends average to slightly below average."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2024/12/saucony-endorphin-trainer-review-with8.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The 42/34 mm stack and soft IncrediRun top layer provide strong impact protection and a plush feel, but heel touchdown is reported as lumpy/back-weighted and overly soft at slower paces.",
        "Responsiveness: IncrediRun is described as highly reactive with quick rebound and improved roll-to-toe-off from the 3/4 carbon plate, but the dense lower PWRRUN layer makes overall energy return feel somewhat muted.",
        "Stability: Medial sidewalls, firmer lower foam, and the plated platform give steady underfoot support, yet upper structure is loose (especially midfoot/heel) with heel lift reports, reducing lateral/heel security for low-volume feet.",
        "Durability: Ground-contact durability relies on PWRRUN foam plus limited rubber wear patches, with very little traditional outsole coverage, suggesting decent foam longevity but higher outsole wear risk than fully rubberized designs."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/saucony-endorphin-trainer-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The IncrediRun top layer feels softer and bouncier than prior versions, but the dense PWRRUN base makes underfoot feel firmer, blockier, and less smooth than expected.",
        "Responsiveness: Despite the plate and premium foam mix, rebound/snappiness is described as muted, with the shoe feeling harder to bring up to speed.",
        "Stability: Upper lockdown is secure with no heel lift, but the platform geometry is described as awkward on uneven ground, suggesting weaker lateral confidence.",
        "Durability: Some outsole rubber was added, but much of the outsole is still exposed carbonized EVA, leaving potential long-term wear concerns."
      ]
    }
